  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\n\nToday and Sunday...\n\nA few lingering and isolated non-severe storms east of HWY-281 this \nafternoon/evening is all that would be left from the upper-level \ntrough that recently passed overtop of the area. Precipitation \nchances across the far eastern portions of the area remain around 20-\n30% as most of the coverage area should ultimately remain dry \ntoday/tonight. This disturbance has been partially responsible for \ndeveloping the widespread showers that have deposited up to 2-6\" of \nrainfall across the majority area over the last few days. Now the \npattern shifts towards a brief dry, yet cloudy period as \nheights/pressure rises aloft (indicating subsidence and negative \nvorticity advection behind the exiting trough [stabilizing \nfeatures]). The next widespread precipitation chance will not come \nuntil Monday night. \n\nTonight, temperatures will fall as low as the upper 50s to low 60s \nwith weak (<10MPH) easterly winds. The conditions for fog look \nprobable (70-90% confidence) tonight as temperatures will be on \ntrack to near the dewpoints (also ranging the upper 50s to low \n60s). The light upslope winds should also support the fog formation, \npreventing fog from mixing out. The only feature that could limit \nthe fog potential overnight would be from the overcast sky \nconditions. A potential thick layer of clouds overnight could act as \na blanket, possibly keeping temperatures a few degrees warmer than \nanticipated. Despite the uncertainy, high-resolution model guidance \n(HREF members & RRFS) show high confidence of widespread dense fog \nforming across a majority of the forecast area (80% of the ensemble \nmembers), especially across the western half of the area. Given the \nhigher than usual confidence, it seems as if the issuance of a Dense \nFog Advisory may be warranted. Beyond the morning fog potential \nSunday, the clouds looming around for much of the day should keep \nthe trend of 70s to low 80s highs rolling on into Sunday. \n\n\nMonday through Next Week... \n\nCloudy skies prevailing across much of the first half of the week \n(through Tuesday) will keep temperatures from exceeding the 70s to \nlow 80s. Overnight lows should mainly range the 50s to mid 60s with \na few upper 40s possible to sneak into northwest portions of the \narea by the end of the week. Highs after Tuesday are currently \nforecast to peak between the mid 60s to low 70s (recently trending \ndown by a few degrees). Remnants of one of the Pacific hurricanes \n(Polo) will look to be stirred northeast and into the Central U.S. \naround the middle of next week. This system looks to bring \nwidespread rain to the region and will greatly impact the forecast \nfor the second half of the week.  \n\nBefore this system arrives, a shortwave trough ejecting out of the \nRockies could bring the area's next chance for precipitation Monday \nnight into Tuesday. A cold front is projected to sweep by along with \nthis wave, reinforcing the near-seasonal temperatures (low to mid \n70s). Most of the precipitation Monday night into Tuesday will \nlikely come in the form of steady uniform rain, though a few mixed \nin non-severe storms may still be possible. The best guess \nprecipitation forecast range through Tuesday currently is currently 1-\n1.5\".  \n\nThe signal for precipitation looks to continue one into much of the \nmiddle of next week as moisture from the remnants of Hurricane Polo \nget fed northeastward and up to the Central Plains region. Given the \nanomalously high moisture concentration (PWAT values 1.5-1.75\"), \nsteady, persistent and occasionally heavy rain could be possible \nacross the period Wednesday through the first half of Friday. \n\nThe mean NBM QPF between Tuesday and Thursday ranges between 1.5-\n2.5\" of precipitation with a 25th to 75th percentile spread of 2-3\" \n(very high spread ... high uncertainty). The 25 percentile for the \narea lies between 0.75-1\" (75% of members exceed these values) with \nthe 75th percentile lying between 3.5-4\" (75% of the members fall \nbelow these values [25% chance of exceedance]). This high spread of \nuncertainty makes it hard to narrow down the forecast for now. Given \nthe track of the system (across the Southern Plains), the greatest \nprecipitation amounts should naturally lie towards the south and \nconcentrated across locations closer to the system center (mainly \nnorth central Kansas). Much more details will be unraveled though \nthe week as the system's track and precipitation forecast windows \nnarrow some.",
